      "text_raw": "When a clock is avail-\nable, as in all sampled-\ndata circuitry, it can be\nused towards a low-power\nCMFB loop.\nThis amplifier is just a\nsymmetrical OTA.\nThe outputs are measured\nby a number of switches and\ncapacitors, which provide\ncommon-mode feedback to\nthe gates of transistors\nM11/M12.\nThis feedback is not\napplied directly to the Gates\nof M8/M6 to make sure that\nnot all current can be switched in the output devices. For common-mode disturbance in the\nsupply lines or substrate, large transients at the outputs can be avoided in this way.\nIn order to see how this circuit works, it is copied when all transistors are on, driven by clock\nW , and then when all transistors are on, driven by clock W .\n1 2",
      "text": "When a clock is available, as in all sampleddata circuitry, it can be used towards a low-power CMFB loop. This amplifier is just a symmetrical OTA. The outputs are measured by a number of switches and capacitors, which provide common-mode feedback to the gates of transistors M11/M12. This feedback is not applied directly to the Gates of M8/M6 to make sure that not all current can be switched in the output devices. For common-mode disturbance in the supply lines or substrate, large transients at the outputs can be avoided in this way. In order to see how this circuit works, it is copied when all transistors are on, driven by clock W , and then when all transistors are on, driven by clock W . 1 2"
    }
